#!/usr/bin/env python3.11
"""ci-result-capture — every tracked repository's CI results, captured as
signals in the Library's Archives (CQ-455, issue_01M4J1ASMSFFPC6S0RQ6VVVT57).

The operator ruled 2026-10-09 that the Bosun posts all CI results to the Library, the
way PR state already is. Until this job covers it, the Library's own
`bin/ci_result_fetch.py` polls GitHub for the same data riding the operator's
personal `gh` login — forbidden by platform/decisions/0051, the same rule
`bin/pr-state-capture` answers for PR state. This job answers it for CI.

AUTHENTICATES AS THE INSTALL'S OWN GITHUB APP (`github_app.installed_app()`), never
`gh`, mirroring `bin/pr-state-capture` end to end: same App, same org-wide
installation, same read-only posture (`_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS` mints a token
that can only read `actions` and `metadata`, so a bug that tried to write
would be refused by GitHub itself). See that script's module docstring and
`lib/agent-platform/github_app.py` for the shared JWT/token plumbing both use.

**THE TRACKED LIST IS READ FROM THE LIBRARY'S OWN `GET /ci/tracked` ON EVERY
PASS (`fetch_tracked_pairs()`), NOT A COPY OF ITS RULE.** This job used to
rebuild the list itself each pass from `config/projects.json`, reimplementing
the same rule the Library's own `convivy/library` applies to that file
(`ci_tracked.tracked()`) — a copy of a rule drifts exactly as a copy of the
list it once was did (the `TRACKED` tuple seeded once on 2026-10-13, stale by
2026-10-26). Merge admission reads the Library's own list, so this job now
reads that list directly (library#335, deployed as a6dc51af) rather than
deriving a second answer to the same question. See
`platform/contracts/library-protocol` §6.8 for the route's contract: the body
is `{"tracked": [...], "count": n}`, one row per pair naming `repo`,
`workflow_file`, `product` and `on_pull_request`; any answer other than 200
means there is no list, and this job must not capture against a remembered or
empty one in its place — `fetch_tracked_pairs()` refuses the whole pass
before touching any cursor, rotation or signal write (see `main()`) rather
than falling back to an empty or partial list. `dedup_key` absorbs whichever
job gets to a run first while both still run.

READ-ONLY, separate from any write path: the only non-GET calls here are the
GitHub App token mint (`github_app.installation_token`, a POST to
`/access_tokens`) and the Library's own `POST /signals` — no `/repos/...`
write of any kind.

`main()` does not always walk `TRACKED` starting at index 0: one shared
posting deadline covers the whole pass, so a pair late in the list could
starve every pass under a sustained backlog
(issue_01M4XNWSQTKYM42Z7YEG70RFQE, measured 2026-10-14). Each pass instead
starts where the LAST pass's last-delivering pair left off, persisted in
`rotation.json` beside `cursor.json`, so a backlog pair gets the front of
the deadline on a later pass instead of always the tail.

THE SIGNAL CONTRACT MATCHES `ci_result_fetch.py`'s `signal_from_run` BYTE FOR
BYTE, SO BOTH CAN RUN DURING CHANGEOVER WITH NO DUPLICATE ROW: `source: ci`,
`kind: test-result`, `actor: github-actions`, `stream` the workflow's slug
(`workflow_slug`), `refs.commit` the run's head sha (the Library's `GET
/signals/pr-state` joins a PR's CI by it), `dedup_key:
ci:<product>:<workflow-slug>:<run-id>` for a run's first attempt — the same
scheme a retired push used before it (see that file's own docstring), so a
run captured by either path first makes the other's post a no-op
`duplicate`, never a second row.

**CQ-533 (issue_01M5FM5NFRPHFJVD8XFK85ABHP, 2026-10-21) appends the run's own
`run_attempt` to `dedup_key` from the SECOND attempt on**
(`ci:<product>:<workflow-slug>:<run-id>:<run-attempt>`), never for the
first: a run that fails and is re-run keeps its `id`, so without this the
re-run's passing result shared its failing attempt's `dedup_key` and was
dropped as a `duplicate`, never posted at all. Attempt 1's key is left
exactly as `ci_result_fetch.py` and the retired push both already shaped
it, since nothing in this repository's own `bin`/`lib` matches `dedup_key`
by this scheme today to tell this job whether a reader elsewhere depends on
that exact shape for an ordinary, never-re-run result (the Library's own
merge admission, in `convivy/library`, is outside this repo's reach to
check); the unconditional suffix this fix could have used instead would
have changed every row's key, first attempt included, with no way from here
to confirm that is safe.

**CQ-533 ALSO MOVES `produced_at` FROM THE RUN'S `created_at` TO ITS
`updated_at`**: a re-run keeps attempt 1's `created_at`, so attempt 2's row
would otherwise carry attempt 1's timestamp — identical to attempt 1's own
`produced_at` — despite reporting the opposite conclusion, breaking the
field's documented meaning ("when the reported thing was true",
`signal_client.py`) and leaving a reader with no way to tell the two rows
apart by time. `produced_at` is the only field `signal_from_run` derives
from either timestamp; every other field (`refs`, `meta`, `dedup_key`,
`body`) is read from the run's id, conclusion or other attributes, none of
which this change touches.

**THE CURSOR INVARIANTS ARE THE SAME ONES `ci_result_fetch.py`'s MODULE
DOCSTRING DERIVES FROM A PRODUCTION BUG (2026-08-31)**, carried over here
rather than re-derived, because the bug is general to any "poll since a
cursor, in flight runs excluded by a status filter" design, not specific to
that file's own transport (`gh api` there, this module's own
`urllib`/`github_app` here).

**CQ-533 (issue_01M5FM5NFRPHFJVD8XFK85ABHP, 2026-10-21) moved every
invariant below from each run's `created_at` to its `updated_at`, and the
measurement below (2026-10-25) moved the FORWARD FETCH back to `created_at`
while keeping `updated_at` for re-run detection.** GitHub's list-workflow-
runs endpoint documents a `created` filter and has no `updated` filter at
all — it accepts the parameter and silently ignores it, so CQ-533's
`updated`-filtered fetch was never actually filtering: measured 2026-10-25
against a tracked repository with 1233 workflow runs (`total_count` with no filter: 1233;
`updated=>=2099-01-01`: 1233; a one-second `updated` range: 1233; control
`created=>=2099-01-01`: 0), every query after CQ-533 fetched the pair's
WHOLE history, which for a pair over `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP` runs tripped
the pagination guard at every split level `_gh_runs_window` tried, proved no
window, and left that pair's cursor exactly where it was, pass after pass —
no CI result reached the Library for any pair over the cap from CQ-533's
merge until this fix. `gh_runs` reads `created` now, the filter GitHub
actually honours; the bullets below read `created_at` for everything a
FORWARD poll needs (the window itself, and holding an incomplete run's
cursor back so the next poll still asks for it), and `updated_at` for
everything a RE-RUN needs (see the re-run paragraph after these bullets,
and `RERUN_LOOKBACK`) — a re-run keeps its original `id` and its original
`created_at`, so nothing building its cursor from `created_at` alone would
ever see one:

  - `gh_runs` fetches every run with `created_at` since the cursor
    REGARDLESS of status; a completed run is turned into a signal, an
    incomplete one holds the cursor back to its own `created_at` so the next
    poll's forward window still starts at or before it and asks for it
    again until it finishes. Filtering to `status=completed` server-side is
    what caused the original (2026-08-31) bug: a run still in flight is
    excluded from the page that would have caught it, and once the cursor
    moves past its own creation time it is excluded from every later page
    too — gone, with no error and nothing in the log.
  - The held-back cursor uses `created >=`, not `>`: holding to the run's
    EXACT `created_at` and then re-querying with a strict `>` would exclude
    that exact run on the very next poll, the same class of loss one step
    removed. `dedup_key` absorbs whatever `>=` re-fetches a second time.
  - `STALE_PENDING_AFTER` bounds how long one incomplete run may hold a
    pair's cursor back before it is treated as abandoned — excluded from the
    watermark and logged loudly as `stale_pending`, not dropped silently —
    so one run that never legitimately finishes cannot freeze that pair's
    cursor and grow its refetch window forever. Staleness itself is still
    measured against the run's `updated_at` (GitHub's own "last touched"
    field), not its fixed `created_at`: a run GitHub is still actively
    updating is not stuck, however long ago it started.
  - A pair's cursor advances once its whole window is fetched AND posted
    with zero errors (`capture_workflow`'s own `errors` tally) — a fetch
    failure or an ordinary failed post leaves the cursor exactly where it
    was, so the next run re-covers the whole window rather than skipping
    over data this run never actually wrote; `dedup_key` absorbs the
    re-covered rows that already landed.
  - THE ONE EXCEPTION: a pair that stops on a SUSTAINED 429 still advances,
    through what it delivered before stopping — carried over from the
    production measurement of 2026-10-14 (CQ-455): a pair that runs out of
    the Library's per-minute budget partway through a large window hit the
    same 429 at the same point every 900s pass, so its cursor never moved
    and every later pass re-posted the same already-delivered runs as
    duplicates instead of reaching the ones after them. `capture_workflow`
    posts a pair's completed runs oldest-`created_at`-first (`gh_runs`
    doesn't promise an order; this module sorts explicitly), so the FIRST
    run a sustained 429 leaves unposted is also the OLDEST unposted run in
    the batch, and its `created_at` is a safe next cursor: every run this
    pass did not post, posted or not yet tried, is at or after it. The
    watermark `main()` persists for a 429-stopped pair is the min of that
    run's `created_at` and the ordinary watermark candidates below (the
    overlap margin, any in-flight run) — never later than either, so the
    existing invariants below still hold. An ORDINARY post failure (a
    `post_signal` result that is neither `created`/`duplicate` nor
    `error:429`) does not get this exception even when a 429 also occurs
    later in the same batch: `main()` advances a pair's cursor past a
    sustained 429 alone, never past a batch that also had an ordinary
    failure in it.
  - GitHub caps a `created`-filtered run list at `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP`
    results and never errors on the page past it — it returns empty, the
    same silent truncation `ci_result_fetch.py` measured in production
    (`convivy/knowledge#ci.yml`'s 90-day window: `total_count` 2500, page 10
    returned 100, page 11 returned 0). A pair whose window holds more than
    the cap would otherwise have its cursor advanced past runs the capped
    query never returned. `gh_runs` answers this the way that file's own
    `gh_runs` answers it: a `>=since` query that comes back at exactly the
    cap is re-fetched as a split range query (`_gh_runs_window`) instead of
    trusted as the whole window, and a capped range already under one
    second wide raises rather than being trusted, since splitting it any
    further cannot produce evidence it covered the window in full.
  - A SECOND EXCEPTION, added 2026-10-25: a pair whose cursor sits far
    enough behind can need `_gh_runs_window` to split a capped window many
    times over — measured against convivy/library, convivy/knowledge and
    convivy/agent-platform taking most of an hour EACH on 2026-10-25, long
    enough to run past the installation token `main()` minted at the start
    of the run (GitHub expires one within the hour), so every pair reached
    after the token turned over failed with a 401 nothing in the run ever
    recovered from, and the next pass started from the very same cursors
    and failed the same way again. Two fixes, together: `GhTokenRefresher`
    re-mints the token as this run's own GitHub reads need it (see that
    class's own docstring), and `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` now bounds the
    WHOLE run, fetch included, not posting alone — `main()` stops starting
    new pairs once it passes, and `_gh_runs_window` stops splitting a
    capped window once it passes, in both cases raising/leaving a cursor
    exactly where today's invariants already allow: `FetchBudgetExceeded`
    carries the proven-complete PREFIX of an interrupted window the same
    shape the sustained-429 case above already does, so a pair this
    deadline interrupts still advances through what it proved, posts what
    it fetched, and picks up the rest on the next pass instead of being
    stuck at today's cursor forever.

**RE-RUNS, SINCE THE FORWARD FETCH CAN NO LONGER SEE THEM:** a run's
`created_at` never changes, so once the forward cursor passes it, a later
re-run of that same run is invisible to `gh_runs`'s `created`-filtered
window no matter how much later its `updated_at` becomes. `capture_reruns`
answers this independently of the forward cursor: each pass, for each pair,
it re-fetches every run `created` in the bounded window
`[since - RERUN_LOOKBACK, since)` (`gh_reruns`, reusing `_gh_runs_window`'s
own cap-and-split fetch over an explicit range) and posts whichever of them
are both `completed` and `updated_at` after `since` — i.e. touched again
since this pair's last poll, the signature of a re-run. `dedup_key`'s own
`run_attempt` suffix (CQ-533) makes reposting an already-captured re-run
harmless, so `capture_reruns` carries no cursor of its own and simply reruns
this check every pass; `RERUN_LOOKBACK` bounds how far behind the forward
cursor it looks, so a re-run triggered more than that long after its own
original run's `created_at` is this design's documented, tested limit, not
a case this job claims to cover.

Usage:
    ci-result-capture
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import datetime as dt
import json
import os
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib" / "agent-platform"))

import github_app as GA  # noqa: E402
import projects as PR  # noqa: E402
import signal_client as SC  # noqa: E402
import vault_plane as VP  # noqa: E402

# The install's own GitHub App, the same installation `bin/pr-state-capture`
# already reads through (issue_01M2KV16ZRG3N49GRER8Q7545H), resolved fresh on
# every run through `GA.installed_app()` — never a literal, and never cached.
# No single OWNER constant any more: a pass mints one token per distinct owner
# the pairs it is about to walk actually name — see `main()`.

# The token this script mints can only ever read workflow runs and the
# installation's own metadata, whatever the App itself holds — see the
# module docstring's READ-ONLY section.
_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS = {"actions": "read", "metadata": "read"}

# The Library's own route for the tracked-pairs list (library-protocol §6.8).
_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE = "/ci/tracked"

# Same budget as `projects._REGISTRY_TIMEOUT_S` — this sits on the same
# critical path (a scheduled pass, not an interactive one), reached through
# the same client against the same service.
_CI_TRACKED_TIMEOUT_S = 5

# A tracked-pairs row must carry at least these; `on_pull_request` is read by
# nothing downstream today, so it is dropped rather than carried into the
# pair dicts `main()` walks.
_TRACKED_ROW_FIELDS = ("repo", "workflow_file", "product")


class TrackedPairsUnavailable(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when the Library's `GET /ci/tracked` did not answer with a
    usable list: any status other than 200, a network error or timeout, a
    body that isn't `{"tracked": [...], "count": n}`, a row missing one of
    `_TRACKED_ROW_FIELDS`, a `count` that disagrees with the list's length,
    or an empty `tracked` list (count 0) — the Library's own contract (§6.8)
    is that any answer other than 200 means there is no list, and a caller
    must not capture against a remembered or empty one in its place. The
    message names the cause and, for an HTTP response, the status — never
    the token. Nothing in this module catches this; `main()` lets it refuse
    the whole pass before any cursor, rotation or signal write."""


def fetch_tracked_pairs() -> tuple[dict, ...]:
    """Every (repo, workflow_file, product) pair this job captures, read
    fresh from the Library's `GET /ci/tracked` every pass — see the module
    docstring's tracked-list section. Uses the same registry-reader
    credential and the same client `lib/agent-platform/projects.py` uses for
    `GET /registry` (`projects._registry_token()`, `vault_plane.library_url`/
    `library_headers`), rather than a second token path.

    Raises `TrackedPairsUnavailable` on every way the list cannot be trusted
    — see that class's own docstring. A caller must not catch this and fall
    back to an empty or partial list (see the module docstring and
    `main()`)."""
    token = PR._registry_token()
    if not token:
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
            f"no registry token: {PR.REGISTRY_TOKEN_ENV} is unset and "
            f"{PR._registry_token_file()} could not be read")
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        VP.library_url(_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE), headers=VP.library_headers(token=token))
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=_CI_TRACKED_TIMEOUT_S) as resp:
            body = json.loads(resp.read().decode("utf-8"))
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE} returned {e.code}") from e
    except Exception as e:
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
            f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE} failed: {e.__class__.__name__}") from e
    rows = body.get("tracked") if isinstance(body, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
            f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE} returned a body with no 'tracked' list")
    if body.get("count") != len(rows):
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
            f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE}: count {body.get('count')!r} disagrees "
            f"with {len(rows)} row(s)")
    if not rows:
        raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
            f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE} returned an empty tracked list")
    pairs: list[dict] = []
    for row in rows:
        if not isinstance(row, dict) or not all(f in row for f in _TRACKED_ROW_FIELDS):
            raise TrackedPairsUnavailable(
                f"GET {_CI_TRACKED_ROUTE}: a row is missing one of "
                f"{_TRACKED_ROW_FIELDS}: {row!r}")
        pairs.append({f: row[f] for f in _TRACKED_ROW_FIELDS})
    return tuple(pairs)


def _pair_owner(entry: dict) -> str:
    """The owner half of one pair's `repo` field (`"owner/repo"`)."""
    return entry["repo"].split("/", 1)[0]


# TEST-ONLY OVERRIDE. Every test in this suite that is not itself about
# `fetch_tracked_pairs()` pins its own tiny pair list via
# `monkeypatch.setattr(ci_result_capture, "TRACKED", (...))`, bypassing the
# Library read entirely so it can exercise `capture_workflow`/rotation/
# token-refresh behaviour against pairs with no matching Library response at
# all. `None`, the production default, means "read fresh via
# `fetch_tracked_pairs()` every pass" — see `_pairs_for_this_pass`, the only
# reader.
TRACKED: tuple[dict, ...] | None = None


def _pairs_for_this_pass() -> tuple[dict, ...]:
    """`TRACKED` when a test has pinned one, else `fetch_tracked_pairs()` —
    see that function and `TRACKED`'s own comment. Called once per pass, at
    the very start of `main()`, so a pair the Library starts or stops
    tracking always reaches the NEXT pass rather than waiting for a deploy
    to notice it."""
    if TRACKED is not None:
        return TRACKED
    return fetch_tracked_pairs()

# This job's OWN POST /signals credential, the same reasoning as
# `pr-state-capture`'s `SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV`/`SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE` (library#176:
# 120 signals/minute is per authenticated actor, and a capture job posting at
# this job's volume needs its own bucket rather than sharing the shared
# agent-platform one). Until the Library mints and installs this job's own
# credential, `main()` falls back to the shared token and warns once.
SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV = "CI_RESULT_CAPTURE_TOKEN"
SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE = Path.home() / ".orient-control" / "ci-result-capture-token"

# STATE, not configuration shipped with the code (platform/decisions/0059
# rule 3) — the cursor outlives any one deploy, and an atomic install swap
# replaces the deploy area wholesale, which would destroy it if it lived
# there. Mirrors `pr-state-capture.STATE_DIR`'s own placement and mode.
STATE_DIR = Path.home() / ".local" / "state" / "agent-platform" / "ci-result-capture"
CURSOR_FILE_NAME = "cursor.json"

# Which TRACKED key the next pass should start walking from — see the
# module docstring's rotation paragraph. Lives beside CURSOR_FILE_NAME, same
# STATE_DIR, same atomic-write shape.
ROTATION_FILE_NAME = "rotation.json"

# GitHub's own eventual-consistency margin: a run that concluded moments
# before this process read "now" might not yet be visible to the API, so the
# next cursor sits a little behind "now" rather than exactly at it.
# `dedup_key` absorbs the re-covered runs this creates. This is a fallback
# for that gap alone — a run still incomplete when a pass started holds the
# cursor back to its OWN `updated_at` regardless of how long it takes to
# finish (see `capture_workflow`).
OVERLAP = dt.timedelta(minutes=5)

# The ceiling on how long an incomplete run may hold a pair's cursor back
# before it is treated as abandoned rather than in flight. GitHub force-
# resolves every run to `completed` within 35 days; 24 hours is grounded in
# GitHub-hosted runners' own 6-hour job-execution cap plus a wide margin, not
# in any one workflow's observed runtime.
STALE_PENDING_AFTER = dt.timedelta(hours=24)

# The bound a pair with no stored cursor starts from, so a first run captures
# a bounded recent window rather than a workflow's entire history.
NO_CURSOR_WINDOW_DAYS = 90

# How far behind the forward cursor `capture_reruns` looks, by `created`, for
# a re-run of an already-captured result — a run's `created_at` never
# changes when it is re-triggered (see the module docstring's re-run
# paragraph), so the forward, `created`-filtered cursor alone can never see
# one. A week is a practical bound on how long after a failure someone
# comes back to press "re-run" (long enough to span a holiday weekend),
# chosen so this lookback stays small rather than growing forever; a re-run
# outside it is this design's documented limit, not a case covered here —
# see the tests for one re-run inside this window being captured and one
# outside it being missed.
RERUN_LOOKBACK = dt.timedelta(days=7)

# Bounds the WHOLE run — every pair's fetch and every pair's post together,
# not posting alone — the same way `pr-state-capture._RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`
# bounds its own run: this job's own `StartInterval` is 180s; a pass that
# runs long has its next scheduled fire skipped rather than overlapping it
# (launchd.plist(5), StartInterval), so 150s bounds how long a stuck pass is
# retried, not a race against the next fire. Before this covered fetch too,
# a pair whose cursor sat weeks behind could spend most of an hour inside
# `_gh_runs_window`'s own recursive re-fetch alone (measured against
# convivy/library, convivy/knowledge and convivy/agent-platform on
# 2026-10-25), long enough to outlive the installation token `main()` minted
# at the start of the run — see `GhTokenRefresher` for the other half of
# that fix. `main()` checks this deadline before starting each pair, and
# `_gh_runs_window` checks it before fetching each sub-window of a capped
# range, so both a pair that has not yet been started and a pair whose own
# fetch is still splitting stop at the same wall-clock budget.
_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 150

# The delivery-wanting timeout `signal_client._DELIVERY_TIMEOUT` uses for a
# one-shot launchd job that cares whether a write landed, not the tee's
# short budget.
_SIGNAL_TIMEOUT = 30

_PAGE_SIZE = 100

# GitHub's own cap on a `created`-filtered run list, regardless of how many
# pages are asked for — see the module docstring's cursor-invariants
# section for the production measurement this is carried over from
# (`convivy/knowledge#ci.yml`'s 90-day window, page 10 100 results, page 11
# empty). A `>=since` (or split range) query that comes back at exactly
# this many results has not necessarily seen the whole window; `gh_runs`
# and `_gh_runs_window` answer that, not `_gh_runs_page`'s own pagination
# loop.
GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP = 1000

# One more than `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP` divided into `_PAGE_SIZE`-sized
# pages, so a capped fetch actually reaches the short page GitHub returns
# past its own cap rather than stopping one page short of it. A guard
# against an unbounded page loop only — GitHub itself never returns more
# than `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP` results for one `created`-filtered query
# no matter how many pages this loop asks for.
_MAX_PAGES = GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP // _PAGE_SIZE + 1

# A page that comes back 502, 503 or 504 is retried rather than discarded —
# issue_01M4XKXB63T8B4RJNAKFPQN7HF (2026-10-14): one transient 502 anywhere
# in convivy-deck's 34-request split-window fetch threw away every page
# already read, and the next pass started the whole 90-day window over.
# Replays of the same request sequence, both under the job's own App token
# and under the operator's `gh` login, returned 200 for every request, so
# the 5xx is transient rather than caused by the token or the request
# shape. A 4xx (and any other non-200) still raises at once, as before.
_GH_RETRYABLE_STATUSES = (502, 503, 504)
_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = 3
_GH_5XX_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS = 2.0

# How long before an installation token's own `expires_at` `GhTokenRefresher`
# mints a replacement, rather than waiting for a request to come back 401.
# GitHub documents installation tokens as expiring within the hour
# (`github_app.installation_token`'s own docstring); 5 minutes is headroom
# against this job's own request latency between the check and the next
# request actually going out, not a measured deadline.
_TOKEN_REFRESH_MARGIN = dt.timedelta(minutes=5)

_CONCLUSION_TO_META_STATUS = {"success": "pass", "failure": "fail"}


class CaptureError(RuntimeError):
    """A GitHub fetch failed, or a run in its response could not be turned
    into a signal. Raised rather than swallowed, and raised BEFORE anything
    in the same batch is posted, so a partial or malformed page never
    leaves the cursor advanced past data this run never actually wrote."""


class FetchBudgetExceeded(Exception):
    """Raised by `_gh_runs_window` alone — never `gh_runs`'s own, un-split
    `>=since` path, which is always one fast query — when this run's
    wall-clock `deadline` passes before a capped window finishes being
    split and re-fetched (see `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`: a pair whose cursor
    sits weeks behind can need far more requests than one run's budget
    allows). `_gh_runs_window` processes a capped range oldest-first, so
    everything gathered before the deadline hit is a PROVEN-COMPLETE,
    CONTIGUOUS covering of `[since, safe_until]` — `runs` carries exactly
    that, and `safe_until` is the one point `capture_workflow` may trust as
    fully fetched. Everything from `safe_until` to the window's own `until`
    was never queried and must be asked for again on the next pass.

    Not a `CaptureError`: a capture error means nothing in this batch can be
    trusted, so the cursor must not move at all. Running out of budget
    partway through an oldest-first traversal means the PREFIX up to
    `safe_until` can still be trusted, so `capture_workflow` treats this the
    same way it already treats a sustained 429 mid-batch — advance through
    what was proven, post what was fetched, and re-ask for the rest."""

    def __init__(self, runs: list[dict], safe_until: dt.datetime):
        super().__init__(f"fetch budget exceeded; proven complete through "
                         f"{safe_until.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')}")
        self.runs = runs
        self.safe_until = safe_until


class GhTokenRefresher:
    """One convivy-bosun installation token for the whole run, re-minted
    before it is within `_TOKEN_REFRESH_MARGIN` of its own `expires_at`
    (`token()`), or unconditionally, right after a request gets 401 despite
    that margin (`force_refresh()`, called only from `_gh_runs_request` —
    the one place every GitHub read in this module goes through — at most
    once per attempt of that call's own retry loop, so up to
    `_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS` times across one call to it).

    This answers the root cause measured on 2026-10-25: `main()` used to
    mint ONE token at the start of the run and hold it for every pair, but
    GitHub's installation tokens expire within the hour and a pair whose
    cursor sits weeks behind can keep `_gh_runs_window` fetching for most of
    an hour on its own (see `FetchBudgetExceeded`'s own docstring and
    `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`), so every pair reached after the token turned
    over failed with a 401 that nothing in the run ever recovered from —
    the next pass started from the same cursors and failed the same way.

    A plain token string still works everywhere this class does: every
    function that takes a `token` (`gh_runs`, `_gh_runs_window`,
    `_gh_runs_page`, `_gh_runs_request`, `capture_workflow`) accepts either
    one, via `_resolve_gh_token`. Only `main()` constructs this class; every
    caller that passes a bare string (every test that does not exercise
    token refresh) gets exactly today's behaviour — no refresh, a bare 401
    raised as `CaptureError` on the spot.

    Mints through `github_app.installation_token_payload` directly, not the
    thin `installation_token` wrapper, because this class needs the real
    `expires_at` GitHub returns, not just the token string.

    *repositories*, when given, is passed straight through to every mint
    (`installation_token_payload`'s own `repositories=`) — the token then
    covers only those repos rather than every repo the installation can
    see. `None` (every caller in this module today) mints unscoped, exactly
    as before; `bin/check-ci-workflow-drift` is the first caller to pass it,
    so one owner's token cannot read a sibling repo under the same
    installation that caller has already excluded."""

    def __init__(self, jwt: str, installation_id: str, *, permissions: dict[str, str],
                repositories: list[str] | None = None,
                opener=urllib.request.urlopen, margin: dt.timedelta = _TOKEN_REFRESH_MARGIN):
        self._jwt = jwt
        self._installation_id = installation_id
        self._permissions = permissions
        self._repositories = repositories
        self._opener = opener
        self._margin = margin
        self._token: str | None = None
        self._expires_at: dt.datetime | None = None

    def token(self) -> str:
        """The held token, minting one first when none is held yet or the
        held one is within `_margin` of its own expiry."""
        if self._token is None or self._near_expiry():
            self._mint()
        return self._token

    def force_refresh(self) -> str:
        """Unconditionally mints a fresh token — `_gh_runs_request`'s retry
        after a request got 401 even though `token()` judged the held one
        not yet near expiry (GitHub can revoke or shorten a token's life for
        reasons this class cannot see coming). Called at most once per
        `_gh_runs_request` attempt, so up to `_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS` times
        across one call to it."""
        self._mint()
        return self._token

    def _near_expiry(self) -> bool:
        return dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc) >= self._expires_at - self._margin

    def _mint(self) -> None:
        # `repositories=` is passed only when a caller gave one — never
        # `repositories=None` by default — so every caller before this
        # parameter existed (and every test stubbing `installation_token_payload`
        # with that narrower, no-`repositories` signature) keeps calling this
        # exactly as it did before `repositories` existed.
        kwargs = {"repositories": self._repositories} if self._repositories is not None else {}
        payload = GA.installation_token_payload(
            self._jwt, self._installation_id, permissions=self._permissions,
            opener=self._opener, **kwargs)
        self._token = payload["token"]
        self._expires_at = _parse_timestamp(payload["expires_at"])


def _resolve_gh_token(token):
    """*token* is either a plain token string (every caller before this fix,
    and every test that does not exercise token refresh) or a
    `GhTokenRefresher` (`main()`'s own, from this fix): returns the token
    string to use for the next request, and — only for a refresher — the
    zero-arg callable `_gh_runs_request` calls to mint a replacement after
    that request comes back 401, at most once per attempt of its own retry
    loop. `None` in that second slot for a plain string, since there is
    nothing this module can mint a replacement from."""
    if isinstance(token, GhTokenRefresher):
        return token.token(), token.force_refresh
    return token, None


# --- pure transforms (unit-tested, no network) ------------------------------


def workflow_slug(workflow_file: str) -> str:
    """ci.yml -> ci; e2e-stage-smoke.yml -> e2e-stage-smoke."""
    return Path(workflow_file).stem


def _parse_timestamp(value: str) -> dt.datetime:
    """GitHub's run timestamps are always `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ` (UTC, zero
    padded, no fractional seconds); swapping the trailing `Z` for an
    explicit offset is enough for `fromisoformat`."""
    return dt.datetime.fromisoformat(value.replace("Z", "+00:00"))


def conclusion_to_meta_status(conclusion: str | None) -> str | None:
    """GitHub's `conclusion` -> the Library's `meta.status` vocabulary
    (`pass`/`fail`), or `None` for anything else — `skipped`, `cancelled`,
    `timed_out`, `neutral`, `action_required`, or `None` for a run still in
    flight. The caller omits `meta.status` entirely for those rather than
    writing a guessed `pass`/`fail`."""
    return _CONCLUSION_TO_META_STATUS.get(conclusion)


def signal_from_run(run: dict, product: str, workflow: str) -> dict:
    """One completed GitHub Actions run -> one Library `POST /signals` body,
    matching `ci_result_fetch.py`'s `signal_from_run` field for field except
    `produced_at` (see below and the module docstring). Raises `CaptureError`
    when the run is missing a field this function reads (`id`, `updated_at`).

    `dedup_key` carries the run's own `run_attempt` from the second attempt
    on (CQ-533, issue_01M5FM5NFRPHFJVD8XFK85ABHP), so a failed run's re-run
    gets its own row instead of sharing the failing attempt's key and being
    dropped as a `duplicate` — see the module docstring's signal-contract
    section for why attempt 1 keeps the unsuffixed key unchanged.

    **`produced_at` comes from the run's `updated_at`, not its `created_at`**
    (also CQ-533): a re-run keeps attempt 1's `created_at`, so a `produced_at`
    read from it would tie attempt 2's row to attempt 1's timestamp even
    though the two report opposite conclusions. `produced_at` documents "when
    the reported thing was true" (`signal_client.py`'s own docstring), and
    for attempt 2 that is its own completion time, not attempt 1's start."""
    run_id = run.get("id")
    updated_at = run.get("updated_at")
    if not isinstance(run_id, int) or not updated_at:
        raise CaptureError(f"malformed workflow run in GitHub's response: {run!r}")
    conclusion = run.get("conclusion")
    meta = {"run_number": run.get("run_number"), "event": run.get("event"),
            "run_url": run.get("html_url")}
    status = conclusion_to_meta_status(conclusion)
    if status is not None:
        meta["status"] = status
    dedup_key = f"ci:{product}:{workflow}:{run_id}"
    run_attempt = run.get("run_attempt")
    if isinstance(run_attempt, int) and run_attempt != 1:
        dedup_key = f"{dedup_key}:{run_attempt}"
    return {
        "source": "ci",
        "kind": "test-result",
        "product": product,
        "stream": workflow,
        "actor": "github-actions",
        "body": f"{workflow}: {conclusion or 'unknown'}",
        "refs": {
            "conclusion": conclusion,
            "workflow": workflow,
            "commit": run.get("head_sha"),
            "run_url": run.get("html_url"),
        },
        "meta": meta,
        "produced_at": updated_at,
        "dedup_key": dedup_key,
    }


# --- I/O: GitHub fetch, signal post, cursor and token files -----------------


def _gh_runs_request(repo: str, workflow_file: str, url: str, token, opener,
                     page: int, created_filter: str) -> list[dict]:
    """One page's GET, retried up to `_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS` times in total
    when GitHub answers 502/503/504 — see `_GH_RETRYABLE_STATUSES`'s own
    comment for the occurrence this answers. Any other non-200, or a
    malformed body, still raises on the first attempt, with no retry.

    `token` is either a plain token string or a `GhTokenRefresher`
    (`_resolve_gh_token`, see that function and `GhTokenRefresher`'s own
    docstring) — a 401 against a refresher's token mints one replacement
    and retries THIS SAME request once more; a second 401 on that retry (or
    a plain string's only attempt) still raises here, same as before,
    naming 401 as the code. A 401 can recur on a later attempt of the
    retry loop above (after an intervening retryable 5xx), minting another
    replacement there too, so one call to this function can mint up to
    `_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS` replacements in total, never more."""
    current_token, refresh = _resolve_gh_token(token)
    for attempt in range(1, _GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS + 1):
        code, payload = GA.request("GET", url, current_token, None, opener=opener)
        if code == 401 and refresh is not None:
            current_token = refresh()
            code, payload = GA.request("GET", url, current_token, None, opener=opener)
        runs = payload.get("workflow_runs") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
        if code == 200 and isinstance(runs, list):
            return runs
        if code not in _GH_RETRYABLE_STATUSES or attempt == _GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS:
            raise CaptureError(
                f"{repo}/{workflow_file}: workflow runs could not be read "
                f"({code}) on page {page} (created={created_filter})"
                + (f" after {attempt} attempts" if attempt > 1 else ""))
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: page {page} "
             f"(created={created_filter}) returned {code}; retrying "
             f"(attempt {attempt + 1}/{_GH_5XX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS})", file=sys.stderr)
        time.sleep(_GH_5XX_RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS)
    raise AssertionError("unreachable: the loop above always returns or raises")


def _gh_runs_page(repo: str, workflow_file: str, created_filter: str, token, *,
                  opener=urllib.request.urlopen) -> list[dict]:
    """Every run GitHub returns for this exact `created` filter value,
    paginated up to `_MAX_PAGES` pages — the shared low-level fetch both
    `gh_runs` and `_gh_runs_window` build on. `created_filter` is the
    complete, already `urllib.parse.quote`d query-string value to send as
    `created=<created_filter>` — the filter GitHub's list-workflow-runs
    endpoint actually documents and honours (see the module docstring's
    2026-10-25 measurement for why this is `created`, not `updated`).

    `_MAX_PAGES` is sized to actually reach the short page GitHub returns
    past `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP`, so this call's own result length is
    never ambiguous between "exactly the cap" and "one page short of a
    longer loop that was never tried" — but it never returns more than the
    cap for one `created` filter value either way, which `gh_runs`/
    `_gh_runs_window` are what detect and answer. Raises `CaptureError` on
    any non-200 or malformed response that retrying does not resolve (see
    `_gh_runs_request`) — never returns a partial list silently."""
    out: list[dict] = []
    for page in range(1, _MAX_PAGES + 1):
        url = (f"{GA.API}/repos/{repo}/actions/workflows/{workflow_file}/runs"
              f"?per_page={_PAGE_SIZE}&page={page}&created={created_filter}")
        runs = _gh_runs_request(repo, workflow_file, url, token, opener, page,
                                created_filter)
        out.extend(runs)
        if len(runs) < _PAGE_SIZE:
            return out
    print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: hit the {_MAX_PAGES}-page "
         f"pagination guard without a short page — GitHub returned more than "
         f"{GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP} runs for this exact created filter, which "
         f"its own cap is not supposed to allow; returning what was read rather "
         f"than looping without bound", file=sys.stderr)
    return out


def _gh_runs_window(repo: str, workflow_file: str, since_dt: dt.datetime,
                    until_dt: dt.datetime, token, *,
                    opener=urllib.request.urlopen,
                    deadline: float | None = None) -> list[dict]:
    """Every run with `created` inside `[since_dt, until_dt]`, BOTH ends
    inclusive (GitHub's `A..B` range syntax) — splitting the range in half
    whenever one query comes back at exactly `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP`
    results, which means GitHub truncated it rather than that being the
    true count for the range. Deduplicates by `id` across sub-windows,
    since a shared inclusive boundary can return the same run from both
    sides of a split. Also the fetch `gh_reruns` uses directly, for an
    explicit bounded range rather than an open-ended `>=since` query — see
    that function.

    ITERATIVE, OLDEST-FIRST (a stack of sub-windows, always popping the
    oldest-starting one still unprocessed), not the recursive newest-first
    traversal this had before this fix — so that at any point, every
    sub-window already resolved forms a single PROVEN-COMPLETE, CONTIGUOUS
    covering of `[since_dt, safe_until]` for whatever `safe_until` this call
    has reached so far. That is what makes `deadline` safe to check only
    once per sub-window, between querying one and splitting or accepting
    it, rather than needing to unwind a partial recursion: when `deadline`
    (checked before popping the next sub-window) has passed, every run
    gathered so far is still trustworthy, and `FetchBudgetExceeded` carries
    exactly that — `runs` and `safe_until` — rather than throwing it away
    (see that class's own docstring for why `capture_workflow` can still use
    it, and `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` for the run this answers: a pair whose
    cursor sits weeks behind needing far more requests than one run's
    budget allows).

    Raises `CaptureError` — never silently returns a window it cannot prove
    it covered in full — if a capped query's range is already under one
    second wide and so cannot be split any further. That needs over
    `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP` runs created inside the same second; failing
    the fetch (which leaves the pair's cursor exactly where it was, same as
    any other `CaptureError`) is the correct answer to a case this code
    cannot prove it got right, not a guess at one."""
    collected: list[dict] = []
    seen_ids: set = set()
    safe_until = since_dt
    stack: list[tuple[dt.datetime, dt.datetime]] = [(since_dt, until_dt)]
    while stack:
        if deadline is not None and time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            raise FetchBudgetExceeded(collected, safe_until)
        lo, hi = stack.pop()
        lo_s = lo.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
        hi_s = hi.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
        created_filter = urllib.parse.quote(f"{lo_s}..{hi_s}", safe="")
        runs = _gh_runs_page(repo, workflow_file, created_filter, token, opener=opener)
        if len(runs) < GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP:
            for run in runs:
                run_id = run.get("id")
                if run_id not in seen_ids:
                    seen_ids.add(run_id)
                    collected.append(run)
            safe_until = hi
            continue
        if hi - lo <= dt.timedelta(seconds=1):
            raise CaptureError(
                f"{repo}/{workflow_file}: created-filter cap "
                f"({GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP}) reached for a window "
                f"({lo_s}..{hi_s}) under one second wide — cannot split it "
                f"any further, refusing to advance the cursor past a range this "
                f"call cannot prove it fetched in full")
        mid_dt = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
        stack.append((mid_dt, hi))  # the newer half, pushed first so it pops second
        stack.append((lo, mid_dt))  # the older half: pops next, keeping the walk
                                    # oldest-first and safe_until contiguous
    return collected


def gh_runs(repo: str, workflow_file: str, since: str, token, *,
           opener=urllib.request.urlopen, deadline: float | None = None) -> list[dict]:
    """Every run of one workflow — ANY status, completed or not — with
    `created` on/after `since` (GitHub's inclusive `>=` range filter, the
    only time filter this endpoint actually documents and honours — see the
    module docstring's 2026-10-25 measurement).

    Deliberately not filtered to `status=completed` server-side — see the
    module docstring's cursor-invariants section for the bug that filter
    caused elsewhere. `capture_workflow` does the completed/incomplete split
    itself, because only it can hold the cursor back for whatever this call
    returns as still running.

    **Filters on `created`, not `updated`:** a failed run that is later
    re-run keeps its original `id` and its original `created_at`, so this
    function alone never sees a re-run once the cursor has passed that
    run's creation time — `capture_reruns`/`gh_reruns` answer that
    separately, by `updated_at`, bounded to `RERUN_LOOKBACK` (see the module
    docstring's re-run paragraph). `GITHUB_UPDATED_FILTER_CAP` doesn't
    exist: GitHub ignores an `updated` filter rather than capping anything
    against it.

    **A `>=since` query that comes back at exactly `GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP`
    results is retried as a split, iterative range fetch instead of returned
    as-is** (`_gh_runs_window`, over `[since, now]`) — see that constant's
    own comment for the cap this answers. The ordinary, nowhere-near-the-cap
    case (every tracked pair's incremental poll, and most pairs' very first
    fetch) costs exactly the one `>=since` query this function always used
    to make; only a pair whose window holds more than the cap pays for the
    extra queries the split needs, and only that path reads `deadline` (the
    single query here is never worth bounding on its own).

    `token` may be a `GhTokenRefresher` instead of a plain string — see
    `_resolve_gh_token`, used at the one place this module actually makes a
    GitHub request (`_gh_runs_request`).

    Raises `CaptureError` on any non-200 or malformed response that retrying
    does not resolve (`_GH_RETRYABLE_STATUSES`), and `FetchBudgetExceeded`
    (from `_gh_runs_window` alone) when `deadline` passes before a capped
    window finishes — never returns a partial list silently as if it were
    the whole window."""
    created_filter = f"%3E%3D{urllib.parse.quote(since, safe='')}"
    runs = _gh_runs_page(repo, workflow_file, created_filter, token, opener=opener)
    if len(runs) < GITHUB_CREATED_FILTER_CAP:
        return runs
    since_dt = _parse_timestamp(since)
    until_dt = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)
    return _gh_runs_window(repo, workflow_file, since_dt, until_dt, token,
                           opener=opener, deadline=deadline)


def gh_reruns(repo: str, workflow_file: str, since: str, token, *,
             opener=urllib.request.urlopen, deadline: float | None = None) -> list[dict]:
    """Every run `created` inside the bounded lookback window behind this
    pair's forward cursor, `[since - RERUN_LOOKBACK, since)` — the range
    `capture_reruns` checks for a re-run of a run GitHub created before
    `since` (so `gh_runs`'s own forward, `created`-filtered window will
    never fetch it again) but whose `updated_at` moved after `since` (so it
    was touched again since this pair's last poll). Reuses
    `_gh_runs_window`'s own cap-and-split fetch over this explicit range,
    rather than `gh_runs`'s open-ended `>=since` shortcut, because this
    range's own upper bound (`since`, not "now") is already known — see that
    function's own docstring for the split/dedup/deadline behaviour this
    inherits unchanged. `until_dt` is `since_dt` exclusive-in-spirit but
    GitHub's own range syntax is inclusive both ends; a run created exactly
    at `since` is also inside `gh_runs`'s own `[since, now]` window and
    `dedup_key` absorbs the harmless overlap."""
    since_dt = _parse_timestamp(since)
    return _gh_runs_window(repo, workflow_file, since_dt - RERUN_LOOKBACK, since_dt,
                           token, opener=opener, deadline=deadline)


def signal_token() -> str | None:
    """`SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV` or the 0600 file `SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE`, or None —
    `main()` falls back to the shared agent-platform signal credential
    (`signal_client.post_signal`'s own `tok or token()`) and warns once."""
    tok = os.environ.get(SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV, "").strip()
    if tok:
        return tok
    try:
        return SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE.read_text().strip() or None
    except OSError:
        return None


def _pair_key(entry: dict) -> str:
    return f"{entry['repo']}#{entry['workflow_file']}"


def rotation_order(tracked: tuple[dict, ...], start_key: str | None) -> tuple[dict, ...]:
    """`tracked`, rotated so the walk starts at `start_key` and wraps —
    every pair still runs exactly once per pass, just not always starting
    at index 0 (see the module docstring's rotation paragraph).

    `start_key` not a string, or naming a key no longer in `tracked`
    (`TRACKED` can change between deploys), both fall back to starting at
    `tracked[0]` — this is ordering only, never a reason to refuse the
    run."""
    if not tracked:
        return tracked
    start_idx = 0
    if isinstance(start_key, str):
        for i, entry in enumerate(tracked):
            if _pair_key(entry) == start_key:
                start_idx = i
                break
    return tracked[start_idx:] + tracked[:start_idx]


def _delivered(tally: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether a pair's batch put at least one signal in the Library's
    Archives this pass — `created` and `duplicate` both count (a duplicate
    still spent a `POST /signals` slot and proves the pair got its turn
    this pass), a 429 or any other post failure does not."""
    return tally["created"] + tally["duplicate"] >= 1


def _cursor_path() -> Path:
    return STATE_DIR / CURSOR_FILE_NAME


def _rotation_path() -> Path:
    return STATE_DIR / ROTATION_FILE_NAME


def load_cursor() -> dict:
    """The `created` lower bound for every tracked pair's next incremental
    fetch, or `{}` when nothing has been recorded yet or the record cannot
    be read — both mean "start every pair from `NO_CURSOR_WINDOW_DAYS` ago"
    to the rest of `main()`."""
    try:
        raw = _cursor_path().read_text()
    except OSError:
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: cursor file at {_cursor_path()} is corrupt; "
             f"starting every pair from its no-cursor window", file=sys.stderr)
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def save_cursor(cursor: dict) -> None:
    """Atomic write: a pid-unique temp file in the same directory, then
    `os.replace` — a process killed mid-write must never leave the cursor
    file holding a truncated JSON fragment, which `load_cursor`'s broad
    `except` would read as "no cursor at all" and trigger a full refetch of
    every tracked pair (safe via `dedup_key`, but slow)."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(STATE_DIR, 0o700)  # mkdir's mode is a no-op on an already-existing dir
    path = _cursor_path()
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    try:
        tmp.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(cursor, sort_keys=True))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def load_rotation_start() -> str | None:
    """The TRACKED key the next pass should start walking from, or `None`
    when nothing has been recorded yet, the file cannot be read, its
    content isn't valid JSON, or the recorded value isn't a string — all of
    which mean "start this pass at TRACKED[0]" to `rotation_order`."""
    try:
        raw = _rotation_path().read_text()
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: rotation file at {_rotation_path()} is "
             f"corrupt; starting this pass at the top of TRACKED",
             file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    start = data.get("start") if isinstance(data, dict) else None
    return start if isinstance(start, str) else None


def save_rotation_start(start_key: str) -> None:
    """Atomic write: a pid-unique temp file in the same directory, then
    `os.replace` — same shape as `save_cursor`, so a process killed
    mid-write never leaves this file holding a truncated JSON fragment."""
    STATE_DIR.mkdir(mode=0o700, parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(STATE_DIR, 0o700)  # mkdir's mode is a no-op on an already-existing dir
    path = _rotation_path()
    tmp = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{os.getpid()}.tmp")
    try:
        tmp.unlink()
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(tmp, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps({"start": start_key}))
    os.replace(tmp, path)


# --- the capture run ---------------------------------------------------------


def capture_workflow(repo: str, workflow_file: str, product: str, since: str,
                     gh_token: str, sig_tok: str | None, *,
                     fetcher=None, poster=None, run_started=None,
                     deadline: float | None = None, sleep=time.sleep,
                     stale_pending_after: dt.timedelta | None = None) -> dict:
    """One tracked (repo, workflow_file) pair. Returns a tally dict with keys
    `created`/`duplicate`/`errors`/`stale_pending`, plus `next_since` — the
    cursor value `main()` should persist for this pair when `errors == 0`
    OR `rate_limited` OR `budget_exceeded` is true (`None` whenever it isn't
    safe to advance at all) — `rate_limited`, true only when the batch
    stopped on a sustained 429 and had no OTHER post failure; `budget_exceeded`,
    true only when the FETCH ran out of this run's deadline and had no
    ordinary post failure either (see `FetchBudgetExceeded`) — and `reason`,
    the first failure's text (`None` on a clean run).

    See the module docstring's cursor-invariants section for why an
    incomplete run holds `next_since` back to its own `created_at` (so
    `gh_runs`'s own forward, `created`-filtered window still asks for it
    next poll), why staleness is still judged by `updated_at` instead, why a
    run stuck past `stale_pending_after` is excluded from the watermark
    instead, why `errors` is what gates whether `main()` advances this
    pair's cursor at all, and why a sustained 429 or an exhausted fetch
    budget, alone, are the two exceptions. This function has no notion of a
    re-run — see `capture_reruns`, called independently by `main()` against
    the same `since`, for the one that does.

    `fetcher`/`poster` default to the module-level `gh_runs`/
    `signal_client.post_signal`, resolved at call time so a caller going
    through `main()` still gets whatever the module currently names; a test
    passes its own fake directly. `deadline` (this run's shared wall-clock
    budget, covering fetch and post together — see `_RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS`)
    is passed to `fetcher` the same way it already was to `poster`.

    A fetch failure, OR one run — completed or not — with no parseable
    `created_at` or `updated_at`, aborts the WHOLE window before anything is
    posted — the watermark cannot be computed safely without both (the
    former for the cursor itself, the latter for staleness and for
    `signal_from_run`'s own `produced_at`), so the whole window aborts
    rather than guessing. `fetcher` raising `FetchBudgetExceeded` instead is
    NOT this case: it carries its own proven-complete prefix (`ex.runs`,
    `ex.safe_until`), which this function posts and bounds the watermark to
    exactly the way a sustained 429 already does below — see that
    exception's own docstring.

    Completed runs are posted OLDEST `created_at` FIRST, not in whatever
    order `fetcher` returned them (GitHub's own API returns newest first;
    `gh_runs` makes no promise either way) — this is what makes "the first
    unposted run" also "the oldest unposted run" once a sustained 429 stops
    the loop, which is what `next_since` needs below, in the same dimension
    `gh_runs`'s own forward window is filtered on.

    A signal reporting `'error:429'` (`post_signal`'s own retry-to-deadline
    exhausted) stops the posting loop for the REST of this pair's batch: the
    deadline it was retried against is shared across every payload in this
    run, so a further payload would retry into an already-expired budget for
    no gain. `rate_limited` is set and `next_since` is pulled back to include
    this stopping point's own run's `created_at`, so `main()` can still
    advance the cursor through whatever was delivered before the 429. Any
    other post failure is tallied as an ordinary error, clears both the
    `rate_limited` AND the `budget_exceeded` exception for the whole batch
    (even one that also hits a 429 later, or followed a budget-truncated
    fetch), and the loop continues — only a sustained 429 or an exhausted
    fetch budget, and nothing else wrong in the same batch, is a case where
    the already-delivered prefix is still trustworthy enough to advance
    past."""
    fetcher = fetcher if fetcher is not None else gh_runs
    poster = poster if poster is not None else SC.post_signal
    run_started = run_started if run_started is not None else dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)
    stale_pending_after = (stale_pending_after if stale_pending_after is not None
                           else STALE_PENDING_AFTER)
    workflow = workflow_slug(workflow_file)
    tally = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "errors": 0, "stale_pending": 0,
            "next_since": None, "reason": None, "rate_limited": False,
            "budget_exceeded": False}
    budget_safe_until: dt.datetime | None = None
    try:
        runs = fetcher(repo, workflow_file, since, gh_token, deadline=deadline)
    except FetchBudgetExceeded as ex:
        runs = ex.runs
        budget_safe_until = ex.safe_until
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: {ex}; posting what "
             f"was fetched and leaving the rest for the next pass", file=sys.stderr)
        tally["errors"] += 1
        tally["reason"] = str(ex)
    except CaptureError as ex:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: {ex}", file=sys.stderr)
        tally["errors"] += 1
        tally["reason"] = str(ex)
        return tally

    completed_runs = [r for r in runs if r.get("status") == "completed"]
    incomplete_runs = [r for r in runs if r.get("status") != "completed"]

    def _require_timestamp(r: dict, field: str) -> dt.datetime:
        """`r`'s own `field` (`created_at` or `updated_at`), parsed — raises
        `CaptureError` when it is missing or unparseable, since this
        pair's forward cursor (held on `created_at`, since that's the only
        time filter GitHub's own endpoint honours — see the module
        docstring's cursor-invariants section) and its staleness check
        (judged on `updated_at`) both need every fetched run, completed or
        still in flight, dated this way."""
        value = r.get(field)
        if not value:
            raise CaptureError(
                f"{repo}/{workflow_file}: run with no {field}, cannot "
                f"safely place it relative to the cursor: {r!r}")
        try:
            return _parse_timestamp(value)
        except (ValueError, AttributeError, TypeError) as ex:
            raise CaptureError(
                f"{repo}/{workflow_file}: run with an unparseable "
                f"{field}, cannot safely place it relative to the "
                f"cursor: {r!r} ({type(ex).__name__}: {ex})") from ex

    try:
        completed_by_created = sorted(
            ((_require_timestamp(r, "created_at"), r) for r in completed_runs),
            key=lambda pair: pair[0])
        for _, r in completed_by_created:
            _require_timestamp(r, "updated_at")  # signal_from_run needs it too
        payloads = [signal_from_run(r, product, workflow) for _, r in completed_by_created]
        created_dts = [created_dt for created_dt, _ in completed_by_created]

        in_flight_created_ats = []
        for r in incomplete_runs:
            created_dt = _require_timestamp(r, "created_at")
            updated_dt = _require_timestamp(r, "updated_at")
            age = run_started - updated_dt
            if age > stale_pending_after:
                print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: STALE_PENDING "
                     f"(still {r.get('status')!r} after {age}, past the "
                     f"{stale_pending_after} ceiling — treated as abandoned, "
                     f"excluded from this pair's watermark): run {r.get('id')}, "
                     f"updated {r.get('updated_at')}", file=sys.stderr)
                tally["stale_pending"] += 1
            else:
                in_flight_created_ats.append(created_dt)
    except CaptureError as ex:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {ex}", file=sys.stderr)
        tally["errors"] += 1
        tally["reason"] = str(ex)
        return tally

    rate_limited = False
    ordinary_post_failure = False
    first_unposted_dt = None
    for payload, created_dt in zip(payloads, created_dts):
        result = poster(payload, tok=sig_tok, timeout=_SIGNAL_TIMEOUT,
                        deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep)
        if result == "created":
            tally["created"] += 1
        elif result == "duplicate":
            tally["duplicate"] += 1
        elif result == "error:429":
            if ordinary_post_failure:
                print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: sustained "
                     f"429 on {payload['dedup_key']}; stopping this pair's "
                     f"batch here, cursor stays where it was — an earlier "
                     f"post in this batch already failed", file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: sustained "
                     f"429 on {payload['dedup_key']}; stopping this pair's "
                     f"batch here, cursor advanced through what was "
                     f"delivered, the rest re-asked for next poll",
                     file=sys.stderr)
            tally["errors"] += 1
            if tally["reason"] is None:
                tally["reason"] = f"sustained 429 on {payload['dedup_key']}"
            rate_limited = True
            first_unposted_dt = created_dt
            break
        else:
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: post failed: "
                 f"{result} ({payload['dedup_key']})", file=sys.stderr)
            tally["errors"] += 1
            if tally["reason"] is None:
                tally["reason"] = f"post failed: {result} ({payload['dedup_key']})"
            ordinary_post_failure = True

    watermark_candidates = [run_started - OVERLAP, *in_flight_created_ats]
    if rate_limited and first_unposted_dt is not None:
        watermark_candidates.append(first_unposted_dt)
    if budget_safe_until is not None:
        watermark_candidates.append(budget_safe_until)
    tally["next_since"] = min(watermark_candidates).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    tally["rate_limited"] = rate_limited and not ordinary_post_failure
    tally["budget_exceeded"] = budget_safe_until is not None and not ordinary_post_failure
    return tally


def capture_reruns(repo: str, workflow_file: str, product: str, since: str,
                   gh_token, sig_tok: str | None, *, fetcher=None, poster=None,
                   deadline: float | None = None, sleep=time.sleep) -> dict:
    """Independently of `capture_workflow`'s own forward cursor, posts every
    re-run `capture_workflow` can no longer see: a run GitHub `created`
    before `since` (so it is behind this pair's forward window and
    `gh_runs` will never ask for it again) whose `updated_at` moved after
    `since` (so it was touched again since this pair's last poll) and is
    now `completed` — see the module docstring's re-run paragraph.

    Returns a tally dict with keys `created`/`duplicate`/`errors`/`reason`
    only — no `next_since`, `rate_limited` or `budget_exceeded`: this
    function owns no cursor of its own and never advances or holds back
    `capture_workflow`'s. `dedup_key`'s `run_attempt` suffix (CQ-533) makes
    reposting the same re-run on a later pass harmless, so running this
    check every pass with no cursor is safe, if not the cheapest possible
    design.

    `fetcher` defaults to `gh_reruns` (the bounded, `RERUN_LOOKBACK`-wide
    fetch); `poster` defaults to `signal_client.post_signal`, resolved at
    call time like `capture_workflow`'s own. A `CaptureError` tallies one
    error and stops this function's own work for this pair this pass — the
    lookback is retried from scratch next pass. `FetchBudgetExceeded` is
    different: its `ex.runs` is the proven-complete PREFIX of the lookback
    window (see that exception's own docstring), and this function takes it
    and carries on checking it for re-runs exactly as if it were the whole
    window, tallying no error for the budget cut itself. (`capture_workflow`
    tallies one error and reports it on `FetchBudgetExceeded`; the two
    functions' handling differs.) Neither ever raises past this
    function (a stuck re-run check must not cost `capture_workflow`'s own
    forward progress its turn in `main()`'s loop)."""
    fetcher = fetcher if fetcher is not None else gh_reruns
    poster = poster if poster is not None else SC.post_signal
    workflow = workflow_slug(workflow_file)
    tally = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "errors": 0, "reason": None}
    since_dt = _parse_timestamp(since)
    try:
        runs = fetcher(repo, workflow_file, since, gh_token, deadline=deadline)
    except FetchBudgetExceeded as ex:
        runs = ex.runs
    except CaptureError as ex:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: re-run lookback: "
             f"{ex}", file=sys.stderr)
        tally["errors"] += 1
        tally["reason"] = str(ex)
        return tally

    reruns = []
    for r in runs:
        if r.get("status") != "completed":
            continue
        updated_at = r.get("updated_at")
        if not updated_at:
            continue
        try:
            updated_dt = _parse_timestamp(updated_at)
        except (ValueError, AttributeError, TypeError):
            continue
        if updated_dt > since_dt:
            reruns.append((updated_dt, r))
    reruns.sort(key=lambda pair: pair[0])

    for _, r in reruns:
        payload = signal_from_run(r, product, workflow)
        result = poster(payload, tok=sig_tok, timeout=_SIGNAL_TIMEOUT,
                        deadline=deadline, sleep=sleep)
        if result == "created":
            tally["created"] += 1
        elif result == "duplicate":
            tally["duplicate"] += 1
        elif result == "error:429":
            # Named separately from an ordinary post failure, the same
            # distinction `capture_workflow` draws — this function owns no
            # cursor either way, so the distinction changes nothing about
            # what happens next here, only what the stderr line says a
            # reader diagnosing a stuck re-run channel sees.
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: re-run "
                 f"channel: sustained 429 on {payload['dedup_key']}; "
                 f"stopping this pair's re-run batch here, the rest "
                 f"re-checked next poll", file=sys.stderr)
            tally["errors"] += 1
            if tally["reason"] is None:
                tally["reason"] = f"re-run sustained 429 on {payload['dedup_key']}"
            break
        else:
            # Unlike the sustained-429 break above, an ordinary failure here
            # does not stop the batch: this function has no cursor, sorts
            # oldest-`updated_at` first, and a re-run whose post is always
            # rejected would otherwise block every re-run sorted after it on
            # every pass, until the blocked one's run ages out of the
            # lookback window without ever being attempted. Tally it and
            # keep going.
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {repo}/{workflow_file}: re-run post "
                 f"failed: {result} ({payload['dedup_key']}); continuing "
                 f"this pair's re-run batch", file=sys.stderr)
            tally["errors"] += 1
            if tally["reason"] is None:
                tally["reason"] = f"re-run post failed: {result} ({payload['dedup_key']})"
    return tally


def main() -> int:
    argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Capture tracked repositories' CI results as signals, "
                    "authenticating as the install's own GitHub App.").parse_args()

    try:
        app = GA.installed_app()
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    problem = GA.key_problem(app.key_path, label="the install's GitHub App")
    if problem:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        pairs = _pairs_for_this_pass()
    except Exception as ex:
        # "If the tracked list cannot be read, the pass refuses and leaves
        # every cursor as it is; it never runs with an empty or partial
        # list" — refused BEFORE `load_cursor()` is even called, so this
        # path never writes a cursor or rotation file at all.
        print(f"ci-result-capture: the tracked pairs could not be read "
             f"({type(ex).__name__}: {ex}); refusing this pass", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    try:
        jwt = GA.app_jwt(app.key_path, app.app_id)
    except GA.Unreadable as e:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # One read-only `GhTokenRefresher` per distinct owner the pairs this
    # pass is about to walk actually name (CQ-455's own single
    # `OWNER = "convivy"` could not reach a repo owned elsewhere, even once
    # the registry started tracking CI for one). An owner whose installation
    # cannot be resolved, or whose token mint fails, loses only that
    # owner's pairs this pass — see the per-pair loop below, which looks
    # its token up by owner and skips a pair with none, leaving that
    # pair's cursor exactly where it was.
    gh_tokens: dict[str, GhTokenRefresher] = {}
    for owner in sorted({_pair_owner(p) for p in pairs}):
        try:
            inst = GA.installation_for_owner(jwt, owner, label="the install's GitHub App")
            inst_id = str(inst.get("id") or "")
            if not inst_id:
                raise GA.Unreadable("the installation carries no id")
            # Minted eagerly, not lazily on first use, so a key or
            # permissions problem for this owner is caught now rather than
            # on whichever of its pairs happens to read first.
            refresher = GhTokenRefresher(jwt, inst_id, permissions=_READ_ONLY_PERMISSIONS)
            refresher.token()
            gh_tokens[owner] = refresher
        except GA.Unreadable as e:
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {owner}: the install's GitHub App "
                 f"installation token could not be minted ({e}); every {owner} "
                 f"pair is skipped this pass, their cursors left unchanged",
                 file=sys.stderr)

    sig_tok = signal_token()
    if not sig_tok:
        print(f"ci-result-capture: no {SIGNAL_TOKEN_FILE} and no "
             f"${SIGNAL_TOKEN_ENV}; sharing the agent-platform signal bucket "
             f"until the Library mints ci-result-capture's own credential",
             file=sys.stderr)

    run_started = dt.datetime.now(dt.timezone.utc)
    no_cursor_since = (run_started - dt.timedelta(days=NO_CURSOR_WINDOW_DAYS)
                      ).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")
    # Every pair this pass is about to walk, by key — a stored cursor entry
    # for a pair no longer in that set (a repo dropped from the registry, or
    # whose CI workflows changed) is dropped here rather than carried
    # forward forever; one still in the set keeps its value regardless of
    # whether this pass reaches it (see the per-iteration `save_cursor`
    # calls below).
    pair_keys = {_pair_key(p) for p in pairs}
    cursor = {k: v for k, v in load_cursor().items() if k in pair_keys}
    # One deadline for the whole run, not per signal — see
    # `pr-state-capture._RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS` for the same reasoning.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + _RUN_DEADLINE_SECONDS

    exit_code = 0
    grand = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "errors": 0, "stale_pending": 0}
    order = rotation_order(pairs, load_rotation_start())
    # The start this pass actually used, after `rotation_order`'s own
    # fallback to the top — the pass-didn't-deliver-anything default below
    # is this, not whatever (possibly stale or absent) value the rotation
    # file held coming in.
    next_start = _pair_key(order[0]) if order else None
    for i, entry in enumerate(order):
        key = _pair_key(entry)
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            # The run's own budget is gone: every pair from here on is left
            # untouched, not attempted and not failed — "never advance past
            # one that was not read" applies to not even starting it, the
            # same as it does to a page this run already began. Rotation
            # (the `next_start` logic below) already starts the NEXT pass at
            # whichever pair last delivered, so these get the front of it.
            print(f"ci-result-capture: run budget exhausted; {len(order) - i} "
                 f"pair(s) starting at {key} left for the next pass",
                 file=sys.stderr)
            break
        gh_token = gh_tokens.get(_pair_owner(entry))
        if gh_token is None:
            # This owner's token could not be minted this pass (logged
            # above) — skip the pair outright rather than calling
            # `capture_workflow` with nothing to authenticate it, and leave
            # its cursor exactly where it was.
            grand["errors"] += 1
            exit_code = 1
            save_cursor(cursor)
            save_rotation_start(next_start)
            continue
        since = cursor.get(key) or no_cursor_since
        try:
            t = capture_workflow(entry["repo"], entry["workflow_file"], entry["product"],
                                 since, gh_token, sig_tok, run_started=run_started,
                                 deadline=deadline)
        except Exception as ex:
            # THE OUTER SAFETY NET (`ci_result_fetch.py`'s own, carried over):
            # `capture_workflow` only catches `CaptureError` — anything else (a
            # transport failure `gh_runs`/`GA.request` doesn't wrap, say)
            # propagates here uncaught. Caught at THIS level and tallied as an
            # error for this pair alone, so every OTHER pair this pass still
            # gets its turn and its own `save_cursor` call below, rather than
            # one pair's unexpected exception aborting the rest of this run.
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {key}: unexpected failure: "
                 f"{type(ex).__name__}: {ex}", file=sys.stderr)
            t = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "errors": 1, "stale_pending": 0,
                "next_since": None, "reason": f"{type(ex).__name__}: {ex}",
                "rate_limited": False, "budget_exceeded": False}
        try:
            rerun_t = capture_reruns(entry["repo"], entry["workflow_file"], entry["product"],
                                     since, gh_token, sig_tok, deadline=deadline)
        except Exception as ex:
            print(f"ci-result-capture: {key}: re-run lookback: unexpected "
                 f"failure: {type(ex).__name__}: {ex}", file=sys.stderr)
            rerun_t = {"created": 0, "duplicate": 0, "errors": 1,
                      "reason": f"{type(ex).__name__}: {ex}"}
        # The forward cursor's advance gate reads `capture_workflow`'s own,
        # pre-merge error count alone: `capture_reruns` owns no cursor of
        # its own and runs this check fresh every pass regardless, so a
        # re-run channel that is stuck (a sustained 429 or a rejected post)
        # must never hold back a forward window that itself delivered
        # cleanly — the merge below is for REPORTING only (the printed
        # per-pair line and the grand totals), never for the advance
        # decision.
        forward_clean = t["errors"] == 0
        for k in ("created", "duplicate", "errors"):
            t[k] += rerun_t[k]
        if rerun_t["reason"] is not None and t["reason"] is None:
            t["reason"] = rerun_t["reason"]
        print(f"{key}: {t}")
        for k in grand:
            grand[k] += t[k]
        # `rate_limited` and `budget_exceeded` are the two exceptions to
        # "only a clean pair advances" — see `capture_workflow`'s own
        # docstring and the module docstring's cursor-invariants section
        # (CQ-455, and this fix's own fetch-budget addition to it).
        if forward_clean or t["rate_limited"] or t["budget_exceeded"]:
            cursor[key] = t["next_since"]
        # Saved every iteration, not once after the loop — mirrors
        # `pr-state-capture`'s own per-repo save, for the same reason: a
        # failure this loop doesn't anticipate loses at most the pair being
        # processed, never the advances every earlier pair already earned,
        # and (now the above `try` catches it) never a later pair's own turn
        # either.
        save_cursor(cursor)
        # The next pass starts after the LAST pair THIS pass delivered at
        # least one signal for (see the module docstring's rotation
        # paragraph) — recomputed and saved after every pair, same reason
        # as `save_cursor` above, so a crash mid-pass keeps the rotation
        # progress this pass already made.
        if _delivered(t):
            next_start = _pair_key(order[(i + 1) % len(order)])
        save_rotation_start(next_start)
        if t["errors"]:
            exit_code = 1
    print(f"total: {grand}")
    return exit_code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
